From 6a26d40a41f40871c5dfba7b953d8e4608586292 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Gambot <56745325+GbotHQ@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 18 Sep 2026 11:06:44 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 3/9] gui: use __declspec, not _declspec, for the
 GPU-preference exports

The two exported symbols that ask the drivers for the high-performance GPU were declared
with a single underscore, copied from the vendors' own example code (NVIDIA's Optimus
document and AMD's GPUOpen page both print `_declspec`). That spelling is an
MSVC-only compatibility synonym; MinGW-w64 GCC rejects it:

    src/gui/Main.cpp:27:14: error: expected constructor, destructor, or type conversion
    before '(' token

Use `__declspec`, which every Windows toolchain accepts. Verified by compiling the file
with x86_64-w64-mingw32-g++ (fails before, compiles after) and by confirming the export
table still carries both names afterwards.

While here, the comment above it promised nothing testable ("sure hope it works out").
The mechanism is only a request - the driver reads the image at process start and the OS
per-app preference wins on Windows 10 20H1+ - so the honest companions are a
glGetString(GL_RENDERER) log and a documented way to opt out. Those are left for a
follow-up rather than smuggled into this one.
---
 src/gui/Main.cpp | 4 ++--
 1 file changed, 2 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/gui/Main.cpp b/src/gui/Main.cpp
index fbe09a1..154feb0 100644
--- a/src/gui/Main.cpp
+++ b/src/gui/Main.cpp
@@ -24,8 +24,8 @@
 
 // This *should* set the OS to use its dedicated GPU, sure hope it works out...
     extern "C" {
-    _declspec(dllexport) DWORD NvOptimusEnablement = 1;
-    _declspec(dllexport) int AmdPowerXpressRequestHighPerformance = 1;
+    __declspec(dllexport) DWORD NvOptimusEnablement = 1;
+    __declspec(dllexport) int AmdPowerXpressRequestHighPerformance = 1;
     }
 #endif
 
